{"id": "19565", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19565", "title": "Lewisburg (Pennsylvania)", "text": "Lewisburg, is in central Pennsylvania. It is a small town that is the county seat of Union County and the home of Bucknell University.\nUnderstand.\nLewisburg had a population of 5,700 in 2019.\nGet in.\nThe town is located approximately 1 hour north of the capital of Harrisburg, and about 30 minutes south of Williamsport. It is approximately 3 hours from Philadelphia, 4 hours from Pittsburgh, 3 hours from New York City, and 3\u00bd hours from Washington DC."}
{"id": "19566", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19566", "title": "Lewisburg (West Virginia)", "text": "Lewisburg is a city of 3,900 people (in 2017) in West Virginia. It was established in 1872, and named for Andrew Lewis, the Virginia colonel who won the Battle of Point Pleasant in 1774. The town was built around a natural spring, which today is enclosed in a stone springhouse near the General Lewis Inn. Today, Lewisburg is a very popular tourist destination due to its historic downtown, specialty shopping, quaint bed-and-breakfasts, and its Civil War connections. Lewisburg is practically adjacent to the nearby resort town of White Sulphur Springs.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nLewisburg is located on I-64 between Beckley and Covington, Virginia. US-219 \u2014 which passes through Marlinton to the north, and Peterstown to the south \u2014 and US-60 \u2014 which passes north of Fayetteville to the west, and through Covington] to the east \u2014 also pass through the town."}
{"id": "19572", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19572", "title": "Lewiston (Idaho)", "text": "Lewiston is the county seat of and largest city in Nez Perce County, Idaho. It is the second largest city in the Idaho Panhandle region behind Coeur d'Alene. It sits across the river from Clarkston, Washington. In 2020, Lewiston was home to 34,000 people. Like Pocatello, Lewiston has a strong working-class heritage. Industries related to timber and logging pursuits have long been key to the city's economy. Because the Columbia and later the Snake River are navigable by some ocean-going vessels all the way to Lewiston, the city has the distinction of being Idaho's only port city, and the furthest inland port in the western United States. \nUnderstand.\nFounded shortly after gold was discovered in the nearby Clearwater River in 1860, Lewiston played an important role in the state's early history. The city became the capital of the newly-created Idaho Territory in 1863, but lost the distinction to Boise shortly thereafter in what proved to be an enormously unpopular decision in northern Idaho. To this day it is not unusual to hear locals lament losing the capital to Boise.\nIt's easy for visitors to confuse Lewiston's two main rivers. The Clearwater River is on the north side, and runs straight into the Snake River. However, the Snake on the city's west side makes a left turn at this point, going into neighboring Clarkston, Washington.\nLewiston is the principal city of the Lewiston, ID-WA Metropolitan Statistical Area, which includes all of both Nez Perce County in Idaho and Asotin County in Washington. \nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe next nearest airports served by more airlines, including Alaska Airlines, Southwest and United Airlines, at more competitive fares are in Boise ' (273 mi south) and Spokane Geiger Field ' (109 mi north).\nBy car.\nBy car Lewiston is accessible by a series of US and state highways which become local streets. WA-Hwy 193/128 bypasses north of town, north of the river on a two-lane highway. "}
{"id": "19573", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19573", "title": "Lewistown (Pennsylvania)", "text": "Lewistown is a city in Mifflin County, Pennsylvania.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nLewistown is 3 hours west of Philadelphia and 3 hours east of Pittsburgh. \nLewistown is 60 miles west of Harrisburg on Highway 322. On Highway 522, it's 45 miles west of Selinsgrove and 35 miles east of Huntingdon.\nGet around.\nThere is no public no bus service, but taxi service is available. The downtown area is walkable and has several shops and restaurants.\nSee.\nThere are cannons and statues that are in memory of veterans.\nThe main landmark in Downtown Lewistown is Monument Square. At the center is a 64-ft marble monument built in 1906. The figures on each side of the base represent the four branches of the military at that time - Navy, Infantry, Cavalry and Artillery. On the south side of the base is a stone that was taken from the tomb of Abraham Lincoln (the only stone that has ever been removed) in Springfield, Illinois. It was donated in honor of Mifflin County's volunteer soldiers (the Logan Guards), who were the first to answer Lincoln's call for troops at the start of the Civil War. \nThere is a mural and plaque can be found on the side of the Subway restaurant building.\nDo.\nThere are few things to do in Lewistown. Bowling, roller skating at the rink, going out to eat, shopping, and relaxing in parks or other areas are the main things to do. There isn't much that stays open after 10PM, even on the weekends, so most of the fun has to happens before then.\nThere is a movie theater (6 screens), community swimming pool, miniature golf, and a public country club. There is a drive-in theater 10 miles east at the Arch Rock Exit off Rt. 322. \nEat.\nThere are a lot of little restaurants in and around the Lewistown area. The two most popular are Downtown OIP and Grille and JP Edwards. The same man, John Pannizzo, owns them. There is a diner, Red's Diner, in Lewistown that has been around for a very long time. It used to be 24/7 but now it closes at 10PM except on the weekends. There are numerous OIP's around the area, all with some similarities, since they started out as a family owned chain. There are also Chinese restaurants throughout town. \nDrink.\nThere are several member wineries, breweries and distilleries:\nSleep.\nThere are many Bed &amp; Breakfasts in Lewistown and surrounding countryside:\n\"Chestnut Manor B&amp;B is within 2 blocks of Downtown Lewistown. "}
{"id": "19575", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19575", "title": "Lewisville", "text": "Lewisville is a city in the Dallas-Fort Worth Metroplex in Denton County in the state of Texas.\nGet in.\nThis city is Located off IH 35E 20 miles north of Dallas and 15 miles south of Denton and from Fort Worth 30 miles northeast via SH121 north and only 5 miles north of Dallas/Fort Worth International Airport up SH 121 which is really convenient for those flying in. Public Transit for the area is provided by the DCTA Denton County Transportation Authority since Lewisville is in Denton County. It connects to Dallas Area Rapid Transit DART in Dallas at the Park and ride station in Carrollton and a Light rail is being planned in the near future along the existing rail line to the east of Lewisville. However using a car would be the best method to get to Lewisville unless you are flyng.\nGet around.\nEven though there is a DCTA presence, a car is preferred. There is a simple loop between Vista Ridge Mall and Old Town Lewisville. Most of the roads are typical of most Texas cities consisting of a grid format. North to South would include IH 35E SH 121. There are two SH121 Business and 121 Toll Road 3 miles to the south and both cross 35 and Mill St splits to the East of 35 goes North or left from 121 Business to go to Old town. To the west of 35 you have neighborhood streets in order from east to west like Edmonds Ln, Valley Pkwy Old Orchard Dr and Garden Ridge. To the East and West you have 121 Toll which forms the southern boundary for three Farm to Market Highways 3040 Round Grove RD where Vista Ridge Mall is located. Most of the shopping and restaurants are located between 121 Toll and 1171 Main St. Outdoor recreation can be found north of these two roads.\nSee.\nMost of the stuff to see is within one mile of IH 35 but most of the attractions are shopping.\nDo.\nGolfing abounds in Lewisville with several Golf Courses in Lewisville and surrounding areas. In nearby Lake Lewisville there plenty of opportunities of water sports at a marina close to 35 even camping at Lake Park in Lewisville. You also have Putt Putt golf and Lunar Mini Golf and plenty of parks to picnic or enjoy.\nBuy.\nThere is plenty of shopping available at both Vista Ridge Mall and antiques in Old Town Lewisville. There is no upscale shopping in Lewisville but nearby in Flower Mound and Highland Village that is increasing and of course the Galleria Dallas is 15 miles via 35 south and 635 east. There is also plenty of shopping in nearby Grapevine Mills and a gigantic outlet mall just 5 miles down SH 121. And of course necessity shopping would include the local, Hispanic and other markets including two Walmarts the better one is on 3040.\nEat.\nThere are plenty of chain restaurants to eat at and most are on the access road of 35. There are a lot of family owned restaurants in Lewisville offering different international cuisines or buffets you can try on a budget.\nDrink.\nThere are numerous bars in Lewisville, with the majority of them on Business 121. \nGo next.\nIH 35E North will take you to Oklahoma in one hour for outdoor recreation. However you don't even have to go to Oklahoma as you have plenty of water and outdoor recreation in Lake Lewisville, Grapevine Lake. Lake Ray Roberts offer plenty of the great outdoors which include camping, Hiking and horseback hiking within a few minutes to the north. There are some ranches to the west in Flower Mound west on 1171 that have outdoor events mostly private and offer outdoor recreation horseback riding as well."}
